  "worked_solution": "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{1}{2}y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/29cf73562152cb8201c8e93545ca7f8416aa8412217cfc9dcdaf87e5b794d971.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{2}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
